<script>
	import Callout from '$lib/components/Callout.svelte';
</script>

Plenty of APIs still speak OAuth 1.0a — and getting the signature base string right
by hand is miserable. Curlew's built-in `oauth1` signer handles the nonce,
timestamp, parameter normalization, and HMAC for you.

## The collection

Keep the secrets in `.env` (marked sensitive) and reference them as variables, so the
signer can redact them.

```yaml
# .env
consumer_secret=!sensitive "shhh-consumer"
access_token_secret=!sensitive "shhh-token"
```

```yaml
# collections/oauth1.yaml
name: OAuth1 API
variables:
  consumer_key: "demo-consumer-key"
  access_token: "demo-access-token"

requests:
  - name: Post a status
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "https://api.example.com/1.1/statuses/update.json"
      query:
        status: "hello from curlew"
    signing:
      type: oauth1
      params:
        consumer_key: "{{consumer_key}}"
        consumer_secret: "{{consumer_secret}}"        # sensitive → redacted
        token: "{{access_token}}"
        token_secret: "{{access_token_secret}}"       # sensitive → redacted
        method: HMAC-SHA256                            # default is HMAC-SHA1
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        "$.id":
          exists: true
```

Curlew folds the query parameters into the OAuth signature base string, builds the
`Authorization: OAuth ...` header, and dispatches — RSA-SHA1 and PLAINTEXT are
deliberately not supported.

## Run it

```bash
curlew run collections/oauth1.yaml
```

```
Collection: OAuth1 API
  ✓ Post a status  200  267ms

────────────────────────────────
  1 request(s): 1 passed, 0 failed (267ms)
```

<Callout type="output">
	Representative output — point it at any OAuth 1.0a endpoint with your own
	credentials.
</Callout>

<Callout type="note">
	The same <code>signing:</code> block can sit at the collection level as a default
	for every request, or per-request to override it — exactly like the
	<a href="/examples/aws-sigv4-signing">SigV4 example</a>.
</Callout>
